<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, viewport-fit=cover">
<title>The Black List &mdash; The No-Stress Netherlands Travel Guide</title>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Oswald:wght@300;400;500;600;700&family=Lato:ital,wght@0,300;0,400;0,700;1,300&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
<style>
:root{
  --navy:#112244;--navy-mid:#1B3566;
  --gold:#F5C842;--gold-dark:#C89A10;
  --bg:#F0F4FB;--white:#FFFFFF;
  --text:#1A2340;--text-mid:#3A4A6A;--text-muted:#6A7A9A;
  --bad:#8C2020;--bad-pale:#FBF0F0;
  --warn:#B36A10;--warn-pale:#FDF3E3;
  --trap:#1A6B5A;--trap-pale:#E3F5EE;
  --tour:#8A5A10;--tour-pale:#FDF0DC;
  --mistake:#5A3A8A;--mistake-pale:#EEEAF8;
  --border:#C8D6EE;--border-lt:#E2EAF8;
  --radius:10px;--shadow:0 2px 12px rgba(17,34,68,0.08);
}
*,*::before,*::after{margin:0;padding:0;box-sizing:border-box}
html{font-size:16px;-webkit-text-size-adjust:100%;scroll-behavior:smooth}
body{background:var(--bg);color:var(--text);font-family:'Lato',sans-serif;font-weight:300;line-height:1.65;min-height:100vh;-webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased}
.header{background:var(--navy);background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse at 80% 0%,rgba(37,69,128,0.8) 0%,transparent 60%),radial-gradient(ellipse at 20% 100%,rgba(17,34,68,0.9) 0%,transparent 50%);padding:2rem 1.5rem 0;position:relative;overflow:hidden}
.header::before{content:'';position:absolute;inset:0;background-image:radial-gradient(rgba(255,255,255,0.06) 1px,transparent 1px);background-size:20px 20px;pointer-events:none}
.stamp{position:relative;z-index:1;margin-bottom:1.5rem}
.stamp-inner{border:2.5px solid var(--gold);border-radius:4px;padding:1.1rem 1.3rem 1rem;background:rgba(17,34,68,0.55);backdrop-filter:blur(4px);position:relative}
.stamp-inner::before{content:'';position:absolute;top:-9px;left:8px;right:8px;height:8px;background:repeating-linear-gradient(90deg,transparent 0px,transparent 3px,var(--gold) 3px,var(--gold) 7px,transparent 7px,transparent 10px);opacity:.5}
.stamp-inner::after{content:'';position:absolute;bottom:-9px;left:8px;right:8px;height:8px;background:repeating-linear-gradient(90deg,transparent 0px,transparent 3px,var(--gold) 3px,var(--gold) 7px,transparent 7px,transparent 10px);opacity:.5}
.stamp-eyebrow{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.56rem;font-weight:300;letter-spacing:.24em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(245,200,66,.7);margin-bottom:.5rem}
.stamp-the{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.78rem;font-weight:300;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(255,255,255,.6);line-height:1}
.stamp-city{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:3rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);line-height:.85}
.stamp-guide{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.82rem;font-weight:300;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(255,255,255,.6);margin-top:.15rem}
.stamp-blacklist{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:2rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--gold);line-height:1;margin-top:.4rem}
.stamp-rule{height:1px;background:linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(245,200,66,.5),transparent);margin:.85rem 0 .7rem}
.stamp-footer{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between}
.stamp-footer-tag{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.58rem;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--gold);opacity:.85}
.stamp-year{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--gold);letter-spacing:.06em}
.header-sub{position:relative;z-index:1;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.75rem;padding-bottom:1.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap}
.bl-badge{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.38rem;background:rgba(140,32,32,.18);border:1px solid rgba(140,32,32,.35);border-radius:2rem;padding:.3rem .8rem;font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.62rem;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#E07A7A;min-height:44px}
.header-note{font-size:.67rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,.28);letter-spacing:.02em}
.intro-bar{background:var(--bad);color:#fff;padding:.85rem 1.4rem;display:flex;gap:.65rem;align-items:flex-start;font-size:.8rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.55}
.intro-bar svg{flex-shrink:0;margin-top:.12rem;opacity:.85}
.disclaimer{background:var(--white);border-left:4px solid var(--navy-mid);padding:.9rem 1.4rem;font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-mid);line-height:1.6}
.disclaimer-label{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.6rem;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy-mid);display:block;margin-bottom:.35rem}
main{padding:1.6rem 1.25rem 6rem;max-width:560px;margin:0 auto}
.legend{display:flex;gap:.4rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:1.8rem}
.leg{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.32rem;background:var(--white);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:2rem;padding:.3rem .7rem;font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.62rem;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--text-mid);min-height:44px}
.leg-pip{width:7px;height:7px;border-radius:2px;flex-shrink:0}
.lp-scam{background:var(--bad)}.lp-trap{background:var(--trap)}.lp-tour{background:var(--tour)}.lp-mistake{background:var(--mistake)}
.section{margin-bottom:3rem;opacity:0;transform:translateY(14px);transition:opacity .45s ease,transform .45s ease}
.section.visible{opacity:1;transform:translateY(0)}
.section-head{position:sticky;top:0;z-index:10;background:var(--bg);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;padding:.6rem 0 .55rem;margin-bottom:.9rem;border-bottom:2.5px solid var(--navy);box-shadow:0 4px 12px rgba(240,244,251,.95)}
.sh-left{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.55rem}
.sh-pip{width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:2px;flex-shrink:0}
.sp-scam{background:var(--bad)}.sp-trap{background:var(--trap)}.sp-tour{background:var(--tour)}.sp-mistake{background:var(--mistake)}
.sh-title{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:1rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.05em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy)}
.sh-count{font-size:.6rem;color:var(--text-muted);letter-spacing:.04em;white-space:nowrap}
.zone{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.55rem;margin:1.1rem 0 .6rem}
.zone-label{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.6rem;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:.22em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--gold-dark);white-space:nowrap}
.zone-line{flex:1;height:1px;background:var(--border)}
.card{background:var(--white);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);margin-bottom:.55rem;overflow:hidden;position:relative;box-shadow:var(--shadow)}
.card-bar{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;bottom:0;width:4px}
.cb-scam{background:var(--bad)}.cb-trap{background:var(--trap)}.cb-tour{background:var(--tour)}.cb-mistake{background:var(--mistake)}
.card-body{padding:.88rem 1rem .88rem 1.3rem}
.card-place{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.58rem;font-weight:300;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--text-muted);margin-bottom:.18rem}
.card-title{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.92rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--text);letter-spacing:.02em;margin-bottom:.3rem;line-height:1.2}
.card-text{font-size:.85rem;color:var(--text-mid);line-height:1.58}
.card-text strong{color:var(--text);font-weight:700}
.card-text em{font-style:italic;color:var(--text-muted)}
.signal{margin-top:.65rem;padding:.55rem .75rem;border-radius:6px;font-size:.77rem;line-height:1.55}
.signal-label{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.58rem;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;display:block;margin-bottom:.22rem;opacity:.8}
.sig-trap{background:var(--trap-pale);color:var(--trap)}.sig-scam{background:var(--bad-pale);color:var(--bad)}.sig-warn{background:var(--warn-pale);color:var(--warn)}.sig-mistake{background:var(--mistake-pale);color:var(--mistake)}
footer{background:var(--navy)}
.footer-gold{background:var(--gold);padding:1.1rem 1.5rem;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:.55rem}
.footer-brand{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--navy);letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;line-height:1}
.footer-brand-sub{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.9rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);opacity:.85;margin-top:.25rem;text-align:center}
.footer-body{padding:2rem 1.5rem 3rem;text-align:center}
.footer-promise{background:rgba(255,255,255,.04);border:1px solid rgba(245,200,66,.2);border-radius:8px;padding:1rem 1.2rem;font-size:.76rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,.58);line-height:1.7;text-align:left;margin-bottom:1.6rem;max-width:360px;display:inline-block}
.fp-title{font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:.62rem;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(245,200,66,.7);display:block;margin-bottom:.55rem}
.footer-contact{font-size:.75rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,.4);margin-bottom:.5rem}
.footer-contact a{color:var(--gold);text-decoration:none}
.footer-legal{font-size:.65rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,.28);line-height:1.6}
@media(min-width:600px){.stamp-city{font-size:4rem}main{padding:2rem 1.5rem 6rem}}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<header class="header">
  <div class="stamp">
    <div class="stamp-inner">
      <div class="stamp-eyebrow">No-Stress Guides &mdash; Digital Companion</div>
      <div class="stamp-the">The No-Stress</div>
      <div class="stamp-city">Netherlands</div>
      <div class="stamp-guide">Travel Guide &middot; 2026</div>
      <div class="stamp-blacklist">The Black List</div>
      <div class="stamp-rule"></div>
      <div class="stamp-footer">
        <span class="stamp-footer-tag">What the other guides won&rsquo;t tell you</span>
        <span class="stamp-year">2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="header-sub">
    <div class="bl-badge">
      <svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 001.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 001.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 00-3.42 0z"/><line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/><line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/></svg>
      22 things to avoid
    </div>
    <span class="header-note">Restaurants &bull; Transport &bull; Tourist Traps &bull; Classic Mistakes</span>
  </div>
</header>
<div class="intro-bar">
  <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
  The Netherlands has one of the best tourist infrastructures in Europe &mdash; but a handful of specific, repeatable mistakes drain budgets and ruin mornings. Every item on this list comes from patterns we have observed across thousands of visitor experiences. None of it is obvious from a standard travel website.
</div>
<div class="disclaimer">
  <span class="disclaimer-label">How to use this list</span>
  These are patterns, not universal rules. Individual restaurants vary, not every taxi tout is dishonest, and plenty of tourist canal boats offer perfectly enjoyable experiences. Use this list to recognise the situations that consistently underperform, so you can make an informed choice rather than a default one.
</div>
<main>
  <div class="legend">
    <div class="leg"><span class="leg-pip lp-scam"></span> Scam / Overcharge</div>
    <div class="leg"><span class="leg-pip lp-trap"></span> Avoidable Tourist Trap</div>
    <div class="leg"><span class="leg-pip lp-tour"></span> Overpriced Experience</div>
    <div class="leg"><span class="leg-pip lp-mistake"></span> Classic Mistake</div>
  </div>

  <!-- RESTAURANTS -->
  <section class="section" id="sec-restaurants">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="sh-left"><span class="sh-pip sp-scam"></span><span class="sh-title">Restaurant Traps</span></div>
      <span class="sh-count">5 patterns</span>
    </div>

    <div class="zone"><span class="zone-label">Amsterdam &mdash; tourist-facing restaurant corridors</span><span class="zone-line"></span></div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-scam"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; Leidseplein and Rembrandtplein squares</div>
        <div class="card-title">The square-facing restaurant ring</div>
        <div class="card-text">The restaurants on and immediately around Leidseplein and Rembrandtplein operate almost entirely on foot traffic from disoriented tourists. Menus are designed to look familiar, prices are 25&ndash;40% above the Amsterdam average for the same food, and kitchen output is high-volume and low-care. <strong>The square itself is worth visiting for the atmosphere</strong> &mdash; sit at a bar for a drink, then walk one or two streets back for a meal. The difference in quality and price is immediate and significant.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-trap">
          <span class="signal-label">Where to go instead</span>
          De Pijp neighbourhood (10 minutes by tram) has the highest density of genuinely good Dutch and international restaurants at honest prices. The Jordaan streets west of Prinsengracht are equally good. Ask your hotel which streets are currently drawing local diners.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-scam"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; Damrak and Nieuwendijk corridor</div>
        <div class="card-title">The Damrak restaurant strip</div>
        <div class="card-text">The stretch of restaurants from Amsterdam Centraal down Damrak and onto Nieuwendijk is the single worst-value eating area in the city. <strong>These restaurants exist to capture the first three minutes of a visitor&rsquo;s arrival</strong> before they orient themselves. Photo menus, aggressive door staff, inflated prices, and mediocre cooking are consistent features. Walking south from Centraal for ten minutes in any direction immediately improves your options dramatically.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-scam">
          <span class="signal-label">How to identify</span>
          Menu posted outside with photos of every dish, a staff member stationed at the door inviting you in, large format laminated cards, multiple national flag stickers. Any combination of two or more of these is a reliable signal to keep walking.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-tour"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; canal-side &ldquo;brown caf&eacute;&rdquo; imitations</div>
        <div class="card-title">Tourist-facing brown caf&eacute; replicas</div>
        <div class="card-text">The <em>bruine kroeg</em> (brown caf&eacute;) is a genuine Dutch institution: small, dark, wood-panelled, unpretentious, serving jenever (Dutch gin) and local beer to regulars. Several establishments in the canal ring use the aesthetic &mdash; the panelling, the dim light, the aged look &mdash; while charging tourist bar prices (Heineken &euro;7&ndash;8 for a 250ml glass) and serving no locals whatsoever. <strong>A functioning bruine kroeg has regulars at the bar, serves jenever by the small glass (&euro;3&ndash;4), and does not have a cocktail menu printed on chalkboard.</strong></div>
        <div class="signal sig-trap">
          <span class="signal-label">Genuine options</span>
          Café 't Smalle (Egelantiersgracht), In 't Aepjen (Zeedijk), and Café Hoppe (Spui) are among the oldest authentic bruine kroegen in Amsterdam. Expect local prices and a genuine atmosphere.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-scam"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Netherlands-wide &mdash; tourist-area stroopwafel and bitterballen carts</div>
        <div class="card-title">Overpriced street food at attraction entrances</div>
        <div class="card-text">Mobile food carts positioned at the entrances of major attractions (Rijksmuseum, Van Gogh Museum, Keukenhof) charge &euro;4&ndash;6 for a single stroopwafel and &euro;10&ndash;14 for a small portion of bitterballen. The same stroopwafels cost &euro;2.50 for eight in any Albert Heijn supermarket. <strong>Stroopwafels eaten fresh off a market waffle iron are the genuine experience</strong> &mdash; Albert Cuyp Market on Saturday, or Haarlemmerdijk market stalls, offer the real thing at fair prices.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-warn">
          <span class="signal-label">The genuine article</span>
          Gouda is the stroopwafel&rsquo;s home city. Any market in Gouda serves them fresh from the iron at market prices. In Amsterdam, the Saturday Albert Cuyp Market in De Pijp is the correct place.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-tour"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; tourist canal cruise dinner boats</div>
        <div class="card-title">Dinner canal cruises: expensive, mediocre, worth skipping</div>
        <div class="card-text">Dinner canal cruises marketed from booths around Centraal and on booking sites typically cost &euro;60&ndash;90 per person for a set three-course meal served on a moving boat. <strong>The cooking is large-batch and the wine is house pour at marked-up prices.</strong> The canal views at night are beautiful &mdash; but a daytime canal boat tour (&euro;15&ndash;18 for 75 minutes) delivers the same views. Spending &euro;90 to eat buffet chicken on the Prinsengracht is a use of money that the Amsterdam restaurant scene does not deserve.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-trap">
          <span class="signal-label">Better combination</span>
          Book a basic sightseeing canal cruise for the canal views, then walk to De Pijp or the Jordaan for dinner at a restaurant where the kitchen has a single location and a genuine menu.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- TRANSPORT TRAPS -->
  <section class="section" id="sec-transport">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="sh-left"><span class="sh-pip sp-scam"></span><span class="sh-title">Transport Traps</span></div>
      <span class="sh-count">5 patterns</span>
    </div>

    <div class="zone"><span class="zone-label">Schiphol Airport &mdash; arrival risks</span><span class="zone-line"></span></div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-scam"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Schiphol Airport &mdash; unlicensed taxi touts</div>
        <div class="card-title">Taxi touts inside arrivals: a consistent overcharge</div>
        <div class="card-text">Schiphol&rsquo;s arrivals hall is actively patrolled by unlicensed taxi drivers offering rides to Amsterdam. They are persistent, professional-sounding, and will quote &euro;30&ndash;40 before negotiating higher during the ride. <strong>Regulated taxis from the official Schiphol taxi rank (outside Arrivals Hall 4, past the sliding doors) charge a metered flat rate of approximately &euro;35&ndash;45 to central Amsterdam.</strong> The NS train to Amsterdam Centraal takes 15&ndash;18 minutes and costs &euro;5.40. There is no situation in which accepting an offer inside the building is the correct decision. Walk past them.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-scam">
          <span class="signal-label">The correct move</span>
          Take the NS train. Platform B at Schiphol station is directly connected to the terminal basement &mdash; follow the NS train signs. Trains depart every 10 minutes. Tap OV-Pay (contactless bank card) at the yellow gate reader.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-scam"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Netherlands-wide &mdash; card terminals in tourist shops and restaurants</div>
        <div class="card-title">Dynamic Currency Conversion (DCC)</div>
        <div class="card-text">When paying by card, many tourist-facing businesses offer to charge you in your home currency instead of euros. This is Dynamic Currency Conversion &mdash; the exchange rate applied is typically 3&ndash;8% worse than your card&rsquo;s standard rate, and the difference goes to the merchant. <strong>Always select &ldquo;Pay in EUR&rdquo; (euros) when the terminal asks.</strong> If the staff member has already selected your home currency, ask them to cancel and redo the transaction in euros. This is your legal right in the EU.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-scam">
          <span class="signal-label">What to say</span>
          &ldquo;I&rsquo;d like to pay in euros, please&rdquo; &mdash; before the transaction starts. If the terminal asks you directly, always select the euro option regardless of what language it&rsquo;s displayed in.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-trap"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; tourist canal cruise boats vs free GVB ferry</div>
        <div class="card-title">Paying &euro;18 for a ride that should cost &euro;0</div>
        <div class="card-text">The free GVB ferry from Amsterdam Centraal (rear IJ-side exit) to Amsterdam Noord takes 5 minutes across the IJ and deposits you in one of the city&rsquo;s most interesting creative districts &mdash; NDSM wharf, A&rsquo;DAM Tower, Eye Film Museum. It departs every 5&ndash;7 minutes, 24 hours a day, and costs absolutely nothing. <strong>Tourist canal cruise operators sell tickets at Centraal for &euro;15&ndash;18 without mentioning the free ferry 30 metres away.</strong> The free ferry does not go through the canals &mdash; if you specifically want the canal ring, a paid boat makes sense. If you just want to cross the IJ or reach Noord, it does not.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-trap">
          <span class="signal-label">How to find it</span>
          Exit Amsterdam Centraal from the rear (IJ-side) &mdash; the water-facing exit. Walk past the bicycle parking to the ferry piers. Buiksloterweg ferry (Pier 2, every 7 minutes) or NDSM ferry (Pier 15, every 30 minutes). No ticket required.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-mistake"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; hiring a bicycle without urban cycling experience</div>
        <div class="card-title">Cycling in Amsterdam: only if you are genuinely comfortable in traffic</div>
        <div class="card-text">Amsterdam has the world&rsquo;s most advanced urban cycling culture, and its cyclists ride accordingly: fast, assertive, with a sophisticated understanding of right-of-way and road-sharing that visitors unfamiliar with European cycling do not share. <strong>Bicycle accident injury claims from tourist cyclists are among the most common incidents handled by Amsterdam hospitals.</strong> If you have not cycled regularly in an urban environment with trams, motorbikes, and dense two-way bike lanes, a GVB tram pass is a safer, equally efficient, and less legally consequential way to see the city. Cycling outside Amsterdam &mdash; along dike paths, between towns in the Randstad, in quieter cities &mdash; is excellent and genuinely low-risk.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-mistake">
          <span class="signal-label">If you do cycle</span>
          Use a basic rental bike (not a cargo bike or electric bike until you&rsquo;ve oriented yourself). Ride in the marked bike lane (always). Do not stop suddenly. Do not use your phone. Trams have absolute priority.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-mistake"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Netherlands-wide &mdash; forgetting to tap out on OV-Pay or OV-chipkaart</div>
        <div class="card-title">Not tapping out: charged the maximum fare</div>
        <div class="card-text">The Dutch public transit fare system is entirely check-in/check-out: you tap in when boarding and tap out when leaving the vehicle or station. <strong>If you forget to tap out, the system charges the maximum possible fare for the route</strong> &mdash; this can be &euro;20&ndash;35 for a journey that should cost &euro;3. On OV-Pay (contactless bank card), this charge appears on your bank statement 24&ndash;48 hours later. Repeated forgotten tap-outs can result in travel blocks on the card. Yellow card readers are at every door and every station exit &mdash; the exit beep is confirmation. Make tapping out as automatic as the tap-in.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-warn">
          <span class="signal-label">If you forget</span>
          OV-Pay charges can be disputed at ns.nl within a set window. GVB (Amsterdam) has a customer service desk at Stationsplein. Keep your journey records and contact customer service promptly.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- TOURIST TRAPS -->
  <section class="section" id="sec-tourist">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="sh-left"><span class="sh-pip sp-tour"></span><span class="sh-title">Tourist Traps</span></div>
      <span class="sh-count">5 patterns</span>
    </div>

    <div class="zone"><span class="zone-label">Amsterdam &mdash; overhyped and underdelivering</span><span class="zone-line"></span></div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-tour"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; Zaanse Schans during coach-tour hours</div>
        <div class="card-title">Zaanse Schans: extraordinary site, terrible if timed wrong</div>
        <div class="card-text">Zaanse Schans &mdash; the open-air windmill village 20 minutes from Amsterdam &mdash; is a genuinely beautiful site. The working windmills, painted wooden houses, and flat polder landscape are exactly as pictured. <strong>From 10:30 to 15:00 on weekends between April and September, an average of 50&ndash;60 tour coaches park at the site simultaneously.</strong> The experience at that moment is more motorway service station than Dutch idyll. Arriving before 09:30 or after 15:30 transforms the visit. The site itself is free to enter &mdash; windmill interiors charge a small fee. Train from Amsterdam Centraal to Zaandijk-Zaanse Schans: 17 minutes.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-trap">
          <span class="signal-label">Timing recommendation</span>
          Weekdays in May or September, arriving on the first train of the day, give you the windmills almost entirely to yourself for the first hour. The light is also better in the morning.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-tour"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Netherlands-wide &mdash; generic tulip souvenirs</div>
        <div class="card-title">Wooden clogs and painted tulip merchandise: rarely Dutch-made</div>
        <div class="card-text">The wooden clogs (<em>klompen</em>) and hand-painted Delft-style ceramic tulips sold in tourist shops throughout Amsterdam are overwhelmingly manufactured in Asia and are not Dutch in any meaningful sense. <strong>Authentic Delft Blue porcelain carries the signature of the Porceleyne Fles factory in Delft and is correspondingly more expensive.</strong> Visiting the Royal Delft factory in Delft itself (with museum, &euro;16) is the correct way to understand and buy genuine Delft ware. Dutch-made clogs exist and are sold by specialist shops, but require verification of origin.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-trap">
          <span class="signal-label">Genuine alternatives</span>
          Royal Delft (De Porceleyne Fles), Delft city centre, has authenticated pieces from &euro;20. The Delft factory visit is one of the Netherlands&rsquo; best-value cultural experiences.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-scam"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; unofficial &ldquo;Heineken Experience&rdquo; ticket sellers</div>
        <div class="card-title">Third-party attraction tickets: always buy direct</div>
        <div class="card-text">Ticket resellers operating near Rijksmuseum, the Heineken Experience, and on Damrak sell tickets at markup (&euro;3&ndash;8 above face value) under the guise of saving queue time. The queue skip they advertise is either non-existent (the ticket is the same standard ticket) or available more cheaply through the official website. <strong>Buy all major attraction tickets directly from the official website.</strong> Heineken Experience: heineken-experience.com. Rijksmuseum: rijksmuseum.nl. Van Gogh Museum: vangoghmuseum.nl. Anne Frank House: annefrank.org. Advance booking on official sites also guarantees genuine timed-entry.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-scam">
          <span class="signal-label">Simple rule</span>
          If a person on the street or in a booth offers you attraction tickets, walk past. Book on the attraction&rsquo;s own .nl or .org website. This also protects against counterfeit tickets, which exist.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-tour"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; Red Light District walking tours during peak hours</div>
        <div class="card-title">Guided Red Light District tours: the district objects to them</div>
        <div class="card-text">The Wallen (Red Light District) is a functioning residential and working neighbourhood. Amsterdam has progressively restricted group tours through the district due to the discomfort caused to workers and residents by large groups of tourists treating the area as entertainment. <strong>Touring the area with a group of 15 people following a guide with an umbrella at 22:00 on a Friday is the version of the visit the city is actively trying to end.</strong> Walking through the area quietly and respectfully as an individual is entirely different. The area&rsquo;s genuine history &mdash; medieval harbour district, oldest part of Amsterdam &mdash; is more interesting than the walking-tour framing.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-warn">
          <span class="signal-label">Context</span>
          Amsterdam city council has introduced restrictions on commercial evening tours through the Wallen. Check current regulations at amsterdam.nl &mdash; the situation is evolving.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-tour"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Giethoorn &mdash; motorised tourist boats</div>
        <div class="card-title">Motorboat hire in peak season: gridlock on the canals</div>
        <div class="card-text">Giethoorn&rsquo;s canals are genuinely beautiful and the village is worth visiting. The problem is not the place but the timing and transport mode: <strong>hiring a petrol-powered motorboat on a summer Saturday afternoon and navigating canals packed with 200 other motorboats is loud, frustrating, and nothing like the postcard.</strong> Electric whisper boats or punts allow the quiet that makes Giethoorn work. Alternatively, the cycling path around the village is free, fast, and empty relative to the water. Visit on a weekday in May, June, or September for the experience that the photographs show.</div>
        <div class="signal sig-trap">
          <span class="signal-label">Better choice</span>
          Book an electric &ldquo;whisperboat&rdquo; (fluisterboot) if you want to be on the water. Arrive on the first morning bus from Steenwijk (Bus 70) before the day-tripper coaches arrive around 11:00.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CLASSIC MISTAKES -->
  <section class="section" id="sec-mistakes">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="sh-left"><span class="sh-pip sp-mistake"></span><span class="sh-title">Classic Mistakes</span></div>
      <span class="sh-count">7 patterns</span>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-mistake"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Netherlands-wide &mdash; itinerary error</div>
        <div class="card-title">Treating &ldquo;Netherlands&rdquo; and &ldquo;Amsterdam&rdquo; as the same destination</div>
        <div class="card-text">A significant proportion of visitors to the Netherlands spend 4&ndash;7 days entirely within Amsterdam and leave without seeing the country. <strong>Rotterdam (20 minutes from Amsterdam by Intercity) is one of Europe&rsquo;s great architectural cities</strong> &mdash; rebuilt after 1940 into a showcase of contemporary design with no parallel in Northern Europe. The Hague has the Mauritshuis and the governmental quarter. Haarlem is the medieval Dutch city Amsterdam was before mass tourism. Delft is 45 minutes from Amsterdam and unchanged in its canal character. Maastricht is entirely different in culture, architecture, and cuisine. The Dutch rail network makes a multi-city stay genuinely easy to plan.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-mistake"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; Anne Frank House</div>
        <div class="card-title">Not booking the Anne Frank House months in advance</div>
        <div class="card-text">The Anne Frank House has operated mandatory advance booking (no walk-ups) for years. Tickets release 2 months ahead to the day at 09:00 Amsterdam time. Peak season slots (March&ndash;October, all weekends) sell out within minutes of release. <strong>The number of visitors who arrive at the Anne Frank House without a ticket and are turned away is, by the museum&rsquo;s own count, extremely high.</strong> This is not a situation where arriving early or queuing helps &mdash; there is no queue. There is no walk-up window. Book at annefrank.org the moment your travel dates are confirmed. This is the single most important booking action for an Amsterdam trip.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-mistake"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Netherlands-wide &mdash; museum planning</div>
        <div class="card-title">Planning museum visits without checking closing days</div>
        <div class="card-text">Several of the Netherlands&rsquo; most important museums close on Mondays. <strong>The Mauritshuis in The Hague closes on Mondays. The Stedelijk Museum in Amsterdam closes on Tuesdays</strong> (an unusual pattern). Most municipal museums in smaller cities (Haarlem, Leiden, Utrecht) close Mondays. The Rijksmuseum and Van Gogh Museum are open daily but reduce hours in winter. Check opening times on the museum&rsquo;s own website 48 hours before visiting &mdash; maintenance closures, private events, and temporary floor closures are more common than standard travel guides acknowledge.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-mistake"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Netherlands-wide &mdash; restaurant timing</div>
        <div class="card-title">Attempting to eat dinner before 18:00</div>
        <div class="card-text">Dutch restaurants typically do not open for dinner service until 17:30 or 18:00, and many do not reach full service until 18:30. <strong>Arriving at a well-regarded Dutch restaurant at 17:00 expecting dinner will result in being told to return later</strong> &mdash; or, in tourist areas, being seated in a restaurant that opened early specifically because international visitors expect it to be open, which is itself a mild signal. Dutch lunch service ends sharply at 14:00&ndash;14:30. The gap between 14:30 and 17:30 is the bakery, coffee shop, or snack window &mdash; not a sit-down restaurant window.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-mistake"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; Sunday planning</div>
        <div class="card-title">Not checking what&rsquo;s open and closed on Sundays</div>
        <div class="card-text">Amsterdam has relatively liberal Sunday trading laws compared to the rest of the Netherlands &mdash; most shops and all major museums are open. However, outside Amsterdam, Sunday trading is more restricted. <strong>In Rotterdam, The Hague, Haarlem, Leiden, and Utrecht, many independent shops are closed Sunday morning</strong> and some close all day. Government offices, banks, and public services are universally closed. Market days (which are the best reason to be in most Dutch towns) run predominantly on Saturdays. Plan city visits to coincide with Saturday if market access matters to your itinerary.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-mistake"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Amsterdam &mdash; hotel location</div>
        <div class="card-title">Booking accommodation based on &ldquo;near the centre&rdquo; without understanding Amsterdam&rsquo;s geography</div>
        <div class="card-text">&ldquo;Central Amsterdam&rdquo; is a marketing description that covers several distinct areas with very different characters. <strong>Hotels marketed as &ldquo;near Amsterdam Centraal station&rdquo; can sit in the Damrak corridor</strong> &mdash; noisy, tourist-saturated, and poor value for money. The canal ring (Jordaan, Grachtengordel) is a 10&ndash;20 minute walk from Centraal and offers a genuinely different quality of environment at similar or lower price points. Museumplein-area accommodation puts the major museums at your doorstep. De Pijp is local, lively, and cheaper. Understand the neighbourhood before booking, not after arriving.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-bar cb-mistake"></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="card-place">Netherlands-wide &mdash; seasonal planning</div>
        <div class="card-title">Visiting Keukenhof in March or June expecting tulips</div>
        <div class="card-text">Keukenhof opens in mid-March and closes in mid-May. <strong>Early March visitors frequently find that the tulip fields outside the park are bare or just emerging</strong> &mdash; the large outdoor tulip fields between Lisse and Hillegom reach peak colour in the last week of April, not in March. Inside Keukenhof, the greenhouse displays compensate, but the iconic tulip field photographs require late April timing. June visitors find the park closed and the fields ploughed. If tulip fields (rather than the garden) are the goal, visit between 20 April and 5 May, confirmed annually at keukenhof.nl/en/bloom-calendar.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
